    shortDescription:
      "Austenitic nickel-iron-chromium-based alloy pipes developed specifically for maximum resistance to acid attack. Features nickel (32-38%), chromium (19-21%), with niobium stabilization and copper additions. Manufactured to ASTM B167, B464 and ASME B729, SB464 standards.",

    technicalOverview:
      "Alloy 20, technically designated as UNS N08020 and often referred to as 'Carpenter 20,' is an austenitic, nickel-iron-chromium-based alloy developed specifically for maximum resistance to acid attack. Its chemical composition is a precise balance of nickel (32% to 38%), chromium (19% to 21%), and copper, with the critical addition of niobium (columbium) for stabilization. The inclusion of niobium prevents intergranular corrosion by stabilizing the material against the precipitation of chromium carbides during welding procedures. The alloy is engineered to combine excellent corrosion resistance with high mechanical properties and relatively easy fabrication. It maintains a stable austenitic structure, providing good ductility and toughness. While it is technically classified as a stainless steel in some standards due to its iron content, its high nickel and chromium levels often lead to it being grouped with high-performance nickel alloys. This balanced metallurgy ensures that the piping systems remain structurally sound and resistant to internal embrittlement across a wide range of industrial temperatures.",

    gradeDetails: {
      standardGrades: {
        "Alloy 20":
          "An austenitic nickel-iron-chromium-based alloy (UNS N08020 / 2.4660) developed specifically for maximum resistance to acid attack. Features nickel (32-38%), chromium (19-21%), copper, and niobium stabilization. Virtually immune to chloride-ion stress corrosion cracking and offers exceptional resistance to sulfuric acid in concentrations ranging from 20% to 40% at boiling temperatures.",
      },
      specializedCorrosion: {
        "Sulfuric Acid Resistance":
          "Exceptional resistance to sulfuric acid, particularly in concentrations ranging from 20% to 40% at boiling temperatures. The addition of copper provides a robust defense against sulfuric acid, while chromium ensures stability in oxidizing environments.",
        "Chloride Resistance":
          "Virtually immune to chloride-ion stress corrosion cracking, which is a common failure point for standard 300-series stainless steels in chemical processing. Excellent resistance to pitting and crevice corrosion in chloride-containing media.",
        "Acid Resistance":
          "Excellent resistance to phosphoric acid, nitric acid, and various organic acids. The protective passive film formed on the pipe surface is highly resistant to aggressive chemical attack.",
      },
    },

