  title: "Hastelloy Tubes Supplier – C22, C276, B2, B3, C2000, C59, C4 & HN",
  shortDescription: "Premium Hastelloy tubes in grades C276, C22, B2, B3, C2000, C59, C4, and HN. High-performance nickel-molybdenum-chromium superalloy piping solutions engineered to withstand the most aggressive chemical environments, offering exceptional resistance to pitting, stress-corrosion cracking, and a wide range of volatile chemical media. Manufactured to ASTM B619, B622, B626 and ASME SB619, SB622, SB626 standards.",
  
  technicalOverview: "Hastelloy C276 (UNS N10276) is the industry standard for aggressive environments, exceptionally resistant to wet chlorine gas, hypochlorites, and chlorine dioxide. It is a primary choice for heat exchanger tubing in pollution control equipment and chemical processing. Hastelloy C22 (UNS N06022) offers better overall corrosion resistance than C276 in oxidizing environments with superior protection against localized corrosion, frequently used in pharmaceutical and biotech instrumentation. Hastelloy C2000 (UNS N06200) is a unique alloy with deliberate copper additions, providing maximum versatility in both oxidizing and reducing environments, excelling specifically in sulfuric acid service. Hastelloy C59, C4, and C-4 are specialized grades optimized for thermal stability and resistance to grain-boundary precipitation. The B-series alloys are specifically engineered for resistance against reducing environments, particularly hydrochloric acid (HCl). Hastelloy B2 (UNS N10665) is a nickel-molybdenum alloy with significant resistance to hydrochloric acid at all concentrations and temperatures. Hastelloy B3 (UNS N10675) is an improved version of B2 with significantly enhanced thermal stability, making fabrication and welding of tubes easier while maintaining the same high level of resistance to reducing acids. Hastelloy N (UNS N10003), also known as Alloy N, was developed specifically for service in molten fluoride salts, maintaining excellent oxidation resistance and mechanical properties at temperatures up to 704°C (1300°F).",

  materialGroup: "Nickel-Molybdenum-Chromium Superalloys",
